\begin{proof}
	Since there is only one Beauville group of order $3^5$ \cite{BBF}, $F/\gamma_4(F)$ is necessarily isomorphic to $\No/\gamma_4(\No)$. Now suppose that $F/N\cong \No/ \WW$ where $\gamma_n(F)\leq N<\gamma_{n-1}(F)$ for $n\geq5$ and $F/N$ is a Beauville group. Since $F/N$ is of class $n-1$ and $\WW$ lies between two consecutive terms of the lower central series, we have $\gamma_n(\No)\leq \WW < \gamma_{n-1}(\No)$. Note that if $n=5$ then $\No_7=\gamma_5(\No)\leq \WW < \gamma_4(\No)=\No_6$ and so $\WW=\gamma_5(\No)$. If $n>5$ then $\WW \leq \gamma_5(\No)$. Consequently the isomorphism  $F/N \cong \No/\WW$ implies that $F/\gamma_5(F)N \cong \No/ \gamma_5(\No)$. We next show that this is not possible.
	
	Note that by (\ref{lcs}), we have $\gamma_2(\No)=\No_3$ and by (\ref{power subgroup}), $\No_3^3=\No_9$. Thus the exponent of $\gamma_2(\No/\gamma_5(\No))$ is $3$. On the other hand, as in the proof of Theorem \ref{p=3}, there is an epimorphism from $F/\gamma_5(F)N$ to a $p$-group of maximal class $P$ of order $3^5$ with $\exp P'=3^2$. Thus $\No/\gamma_5(\No)$ cannot be isomorphic to $F /\gamma_5(F)N$.
	
\end{proof}








